{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"On a random 3-regular graph with asynchronous majority dynamics from a uniform random two-coloring, the probability of reaching unanimous consensus as n grows tends to a constant strictly between 0 and 1 (bounded away from both 0 and 1). The obstruction structures are locally stable mixed configurations: colorings in which every vertex agrees with at least two of its three neighbors, so no vertex ever changes its opinion. In any minimal such mixed configuration the disagreeing edges form a matching (each vertex has exactly one neighbor of the opposite color and two of the same), i.e. the vertex set decomposes into monochromatic induced components pairwise joined by a perfect matching of cross edges. Their prevalence follows a falsifiable quantitative law: the dynamics converges whp to a locally stable configuration whose expected number of disagreeing-edge pairs is Θ(n), proportional to n with a positive constant times n density; consensus (zero pairs) is a large-deviation event with probability strictly between 0 and 1, so the consensus probability approaches a nondegenerate constant, and the count of dissenting pairs per vertex converges to a Poisson-free positive constant independent of n.","counterconditions":["If the disagreeing-edge matching can always be dissolved by correlated chains of updates (a vertex with two same-colored neighbors of the opposite color flipping), the bounded-below claim fails and consensus probability tends to 1.","If the expected number of surviving dissenting pairs were o(n) or exponentially small in n, the constant-verdict fails.","If random 3-regular graphs whp admit a global potential argument forcing energy to zero, consensus probability → 1.","If the absorbing set includes period-2 cycles rather than only fixed points, the obstruction characterization must be widened."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Each asynchronous update moves a vertex to the local majority of its three neighbors, which can only decrease the number of disagreeing edges incident to it or create new ones on the other side; on a 3-regular graph a vertex is stable exactly when at most one neighbor disagrees with it. Absorbing states of the dynamics are exactly the locally stable configurations, which include consensus but also mixed states where the disagreeing-edge subgraph has maximum degree 1 (a matching): flipping any single vertex would then put it in the minority of its neighborhood, so it never flips under majority rule. Starting from a uniform coloring, an Ω(n) fraction of vertices sit in stable local patches, and the asynchronous dynamics eliminates unstable vertices in O(n log n) expected steps while being unable to dissolve a matching of cross edges once each side is internally monochromatic; hence the limiting state is mixed with probability bounded below by a constant, giving a consensus probability strictly between 0 and 1 as n → ∞.","optional_refs":["513b3472f223238c0947d9cb812567f2a18bcbc52fdb1edc59b2e89d8de630e2"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The probability of reaching unanimous consensus tends to 1 as n → ∞ (probability of consensus approaches 1, i.e. is 1 - o(1)): no locally stable mixed obstruction survives on a random 3-regular graph whp. Candidate obstructions are configurations where every vertex agrees with the majority of its three neighbors while the coloring stays mixed; but on a random 3-regular graph, which whp has large girth and is an expander, any such mixed configuration forces the disagreeing-edge structure to be a matching separating large monochromatic induced subgraphs, and random expanders have edge expansion Ω(1), so the boundary between color classes exceeds what a matching can support. Hence every locally stable mixed configuration is a frozen structure whose expected count vanishes exponentially in n, and the consensus probability approaches 1 in the limit of large n.","counterconditions":["If one can exhibit a family of constant-size locally stable mixed gadgets that appear with expected count growing like a positive constant times n (linear in n) on random 3-regular graphs, the →1 verdict is falsified.","If the Lyapunov function is not nonincreasing under asynchronous majority flips, the drift argument collapses.","If periodic (period-2) orbits absorb a positive fraction of trajectories, consensus probability is bounded away from 1."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Asynchronous majority updates monotonically reduce a Lyapunov function (total number of disagreeing edges changes by a nonpositive amount in expectation at unstable vertices). Vertices with two or three disagreeing neighbors flip and locally homogenize; the only trap is a configuration where the minority boundary has saturated into a matching. On a random 3-regular graph with girth growing and expansion bounded below, a monochromatic set with a matching boundary must contain Ω(n) vertices with internally tree-like neighborhoods; but expansion forces |∂S| ≥ c|S| for some c > 1/3, contradicting |∂S| matching-sized relative to min(|S|, |S^c|) unless S^c is O(1). Small obstruction islands (constant-size stable mixed subgraphs, e.g. a 4-cycle with alternating induced attachments) appear with expected count that is O(1) or decays, so whp none traps the dynamics and consensus is reached.","optional_refs":["513b3472f223238c0947d9cb812567f2a18bcbc52fdb1edc59b2e89d8de630e2"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Consensus probability as n grows tends to 0: the dynamics whp gets trapped in a locally stable mixed configuration, because such obstructions are abundant. The obstruction structures are frozen colorings where every vertex has at least two of its three neighbors of its own color; equivalently the disagreeing edges form a subgraph of maximum degree 1 on each side. The simplest and dominant gadget is a pair of adjacent disagreeing-edge endpoints forming an isolated cross-edge between two internally monochromatic induced subtrees. Quantitative law: the expected number of such absorbing mixed configurations reachable from a random start grows exponentially in n (like c^n with c > 1 for a positive constant c), so the probability of consensus vanishes as n → ∞.","counterconditions":["If chains of flips can cascade through a frozen matching (a vertex temporarily in minority flipping and destabilizing the basin), the →0 verdict fails.","If simulations show consensus probability empirically near a constant strictly between 0 and 1 for large n, the →0 claim is falsified.","If the frozen-at-time-zero set is actually o(n), the abundance mechanism fails."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Asynchronous updates fix vertices one at a time; once a vertex has a 2-to-1 local majority it is frozen, and freezing propagates outward from random initial seeds. Since a uniform random coloring already has a constant fraction of vertices in local majority-agreement, an Ω(n) set is frozen from step 0, and the dynamics merely prunes the boundary between color basins to a matching. With positive probability (bounded below, whp realized on random 3-regular graphs) both color classes retain Ω(n) vertices whose neighborhoods saturate into a stable matching, so no sequence of single-vertex majority updates can ever make the coloring unanimous: consensus probability is bounded away from 1 by an Ω(1) amount and in fact → 0 as the number of independent frozen basins grows linearly in n.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Falsifiable-law candidate (deliberately minimal-commitment): whatever the limiting consensus probability is, the decisive quantity is the density of locally stable mixed configurations, and it obeys a clean quantitative law — the expected number of vertices that end in a dissenting matched pair after dynamics stabilizes scales like Θ(n) with an explicit per-vertex constant ρ ≈ 0.05–0.15 (empirically measurable), so the probability that this expected count is zero (i.e. consensus) converges to a Poisson-type limit: probability of consensus → exp(-ρn) is ruled out; instead the count does not converge to a Poisson because the matching forms in correlated blocks, giving consensus probability tending to a constant strictly between 0 and 1 as n grows. The obstruction characterization: absorbing mixed states = colorings where every vertex agrees with two of its three neighbors, forcing the disagreeing-edge subgraph to be a matching whose removal leaves monochromatic induced components each of size ≥ 2.","counterconditions":["If the block-count actually converges to a Poisson distribution with mean growing or constant, the constant verdict changes (Poisson with constant mean would give consensus probability → e^{-λ}, still strictly between 0 and 1 — this variant is admitted).","If measured ρ = 0 for large n, consensus probability tends to 1.","If measured final dissenting count is o(n) but positive with constant probability, consensus probability tends to a constant strictly between 0 and 1 via a different route."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Run the asynchronous dynamics: it reaches an absorbing state in O(n log n) steps whp because each unstable vertex flip strictly decreases the number of disagreeing edges when it had 2 or 3 disagreeing neighbors. The absorbing state's dissenting matching arises from blocks of vertices that were mutually reinforcing from time 0. Correlation between blocks decays with graph distance; on a locally tree-like random 3-regular graph, blocks behave roughly independently with constant per-vertex probability ρ of harboring a final dissenting pair, so the total count is Θ(n) (linear in n) with a law of large numbers rather than a Poisson limit; consensus requires all n blocks clean, a probability that is a nontrivial constant as n → ∞ because block correlations have global components (the final two basins compete), not independent multiplication.","optional_refs":["513b3472f223238c0947d9cb812567f2a18bcbc52fdb1edc59b2e89d8de630e2","a9b051224d4429f2de969113f81f7026c21de0813a4120755d0748d2228a7513"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Counterexample-first structural claim: the correct obstruction characterization must include not only static fixed points but also period-2 orbits, and their inclusion flips the verdict to consensus probability tending to 0 on random 3-regular graphs. Asynchronous majority dynamics on 3-regular graphs is known (Goles–Olkiewicz style results) to eventually enter a cycle of period at most 2; a locally stable mixed configuration in the broad sense is either a frozen fixed point (each vertex agrees with at least two of its three neighbors) or a 2-cycle where a vertex with a tied neighborhood oscillates. However, with 3 neighbors ties are impossible (majority of 3 is always defined), so the only obstructions are the matching-boundary frozen states; their expected count on a random 3-regular graph is Θ(n) — specifically the expected number of matched cross-edge pairs surviving absorption is a constant times n with constant ≈ n·2^{-Θ(√n)}-corrected... concretely: probability that a random coloring is itself absorbing and mixed is exponentially small in n, but the dynamics funnels toward such states, and the fraction of runs ending non-consensus is bounded below by a strictly positive constant, so consensus probability tends to a constant strictly between 0 and 1 as n → ∞.","counterconditions":["If an explicit energy-increasing move exists (a flip that raises disagreeing-edge count), the monotone-absorption argument fails.","If the branching-process freeze probability computes to 0, the bounded-below constant fails and consensus probability may tend to 1.","If the period-at-most-2 theorem does not apply to this asynchronous uniform-vertex schedule, the obstruction taxonomy is incomplete."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Because degree 3 is odd, every update is deterministic given the neighborhood, and the dynamics has no ties, eliminating period-2 obstructions a priori (this is the counterexample-first step: check whether the obvious generalization to period-2 fails — it does). The energy E = number of disagreeing edges decreases whenever a vertex with ≥2 disagreeing neighbors is updated, and is unchanged otherwise; the process is an absorbing Markov chain whose absorbing states are exactly the frozen mixed configurations plus consensus. On the locally tree-like random 3-regular graph, a branching-process calculation on the initial coloring gives a positive probability that the root's neighborhood is already frozen and part of a basin of linear size; by symmetry and whp-realization, both colors spawn such basins, and the final matching between them has expected size proportional to n, giving a consensus probability that is a nondegenerate constant strictly between 0 and 1 in the limit of large n.","optional_refs":["d9ae544b53a02fc6d6edc522f2b5bbd901f112b031737f2094d4edc88277c00a"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Atypical, decisive-experiment framing: the entire verdict question reduces to one measurable number — the probability p_n that a random 3-regular graph admits ANY locally stable mixed coloring reachable from a uniform start. Conjecture: p_n → p* with 0 < p* < 1, and the consensus probability equals 1 - p* + o(1), i.e. tends to a constant strictly between 0 and 1. Obstruction structure: a locally stable mixed configuration is a two-coloring in which no vertex is in the minority among its neighborhood; every vertex agrees with the majority of its three neighbors; the color classes then have the property that each induced subgraph on a color class has minimum degree ≥ 2 (each vertex has at least 2 same-color neighbors), i.e. each color class spans a subgraph with min degree 2 — a union of cycles with chords. Quantitative law: the expected number of such bi-min-degree-2 splits on a random 3-regular graph grows exponentially in n (there are exponentially many, ~ (c√2)^{n} candidates), but the fraction reachable from random initial conditions under the dynamics is Θ(1): the fraction of the absorbing basin of mixed states among all initial colorings converges to a positive constant, giving the strictly-between verdict as n grows.","counterconditions":["If the branching-process coexistence probability computes to exactly 0 or 1, the strictly-between verdict fails.","If min-degree-2 induced splits do not exist whp (e.g. every 3-regular graph's only stable colorings are monochromatic), consensus probability tends to 1.","If reachable mixed absorbing states have basin probability decaying like o(1), consensus probability tends to 1; if it grows like 1 - o(1), tends to 0."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Local stability ⟺ every vertex has ≥2 same-colored neighbors ⟺ each color class induces a minimum-degree-≥2 spanning subgraph of the 3-regular graph, hence a union of disjoint cyclic cores joined by a matching of cross edges between the two colors. Random 3-regular graphs whp contain many such splits (e.g. take any 2-factor decomposition argument), so absorbing mixed states exist; but reachability is the filter: the asynchronous dynamics from a uniform coloring performs a local majority-annealing that lands in a mixed absorbing state only when initial color blocks percolate compatibly, an event with probability that has a limit strictly between 0 and 1 by a two-type branching-process survival calculation on the locally tree-like structure (supercritical for both colors, but coexistence of two surviving frozen basins has probability 1 - 2·q + q²·... landing strictly inside (0,1)).","optional_refs":["513b3472f223238c0947d9cb812567f2a18bcbc52fdb1edc59b2e89d8de630e2"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Speculative fragment: on 3-regular graphs, absorbing mixed states = colorings where disagreeing edges form a matching AND each color class induces min-degree ≥ 2? Check: if a vertex has 3 same-color neighbors it has degree 3 in its color class; if it has 2, degree 2. So each color class induces min degree ≥ 2 exactly. But wait — a vertex could have only 1 same-color neighbor and 2 disagreeing: then it flips. So stability ⟺ min degree 2 in color class. On 3-regular graph, color classes have induced average degree ≥ 2 each; total edges n·3/2; two classes each with min degree 2 on sizes k and n-k: need ≥ k and ≥ n-k internal edges summing to ≤ 1.5n, always feasible. Cross edges = 1.5n - (internal), and each vertex has exactly 3 - deg_int cross edges... but stability requires deg_int ≥ 2 so cross-degree ≤ 1: disagreeing edges form a matching. Consistent.","possible_next_move":"Compute, via simulation on random 3-regular graphs, the empirical consensus probability for n up to 10^5 and the per-vertex density of final dissenting pairs; check whether it converges to a constant and estimate ρ.","why_keep_this":"The min-degree-2 reformulation turns the obstruction question into a clean extremal/structural question about induced subgraphs of random 3-regular graphs."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Does the expected number of final dissenting matching pairs per vertex converge to a constant, and is the block structure independent enough for a Poisson limit at small ρ, or is a law of large numbers the right description?","related_refs":["NEW_001"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}